module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_authored_import_emits_no_use_line_when_its_spelling_is_forked

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data an_authored_import_emits_no_use_line_when_its_spelling_is_forked: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_authored_import_emits_no_use_line_when_its_spelling_is_forked" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "an authored import emits no use-line when its spelling is forked (a module authors `import M { T }` and uses `T` in a signature; the emitted module RENDERS the bare `T` and emits NO use-line binding it, so one emitted module contradicts itself and the target compiler refuses at the reference). INVALID STATE: an emitted module that spells a type it declined to import. HARM: E0425 at rustc on a crate the compiler reported as emitted clean -- the emission itself reports success, so the refusal lands in the target toolchain rather than at any gunbc boundary, and it lands in a module whose SOURCE names exactly one provider for the name.",

    "THE CONDITION IS A CONJUNCTION OF THREE TERMS, measured 2026-09-22 (sharp-bear-756) through the real Rust emitter, and every term was established by an arm that varies it alone. (1) TWO modules in the compiled closure declare the short spelling. (2) The declaration the author NAMED is a NON-NOMINAL ALIAS -- one with no Rust nominal shell of its own. (3) The SECOND declarer reaches the closure through a module the consumer DOES NOT IMPORT. No pair of these suffices. This is stated first because two natural summaries are both FALSE: 'a duplicate short name breaks the import' would put 34 forked spellings in the population, and 'two declarers in the closure breaks the import' would include the case the emitter handles correctly.",

    "THE ARMS, one variable each, every arm PRODUCING emitted output so that no reading rests on an arm that measured nothing. Consumer in every arm: `import std.integer { UInt8 }` with `fn count_octets(xs: List<UInt8>) -> Int`. ARM A, closure holds std.integer only (33 files emitted): use-line PRESENT. ARM B, v2.std.integer added and its bystander imports UInt8 from it (50 files): use-line ABSENT. ARM C, v2.std.integer added but NOTHING in the closure imports UInt8 from it -- the bystander takes OverflowDisposition, which only v2.std.integer declares (50 files): use-line ABSENT. Arms B and C are byte-identical in the consumer, so THE BYSTANDER'S IMPORT LIST IS IRRELEVANT. ARM D, the same shape over a NOMINAL record -- std.termination TerminationProof against v2.std.cardinality TerminationProof, a genuine meaning fork with disjoint fields -- with and without the second declarer reaching the closure through a BYSTANDER: use-line PRESENT in BOTH, so the fork alone is not sufficient and the alias shape alone is not either. THE BYSTANDER DETAIL IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE ENROLLED VERSION OF THIS ARM FIRST GOT IT WRONG: when the nominal consumer NAMES v2.std.cardinality itself, term (3) is violated and the arm is green for that reason instead, establishing nothing about nominality while being cited as the bound that keeps 34 forked spellings out of the population (review 70079). A permanently-green arm whose verdict is decided by a different term is decoration, and the confound is invisible precisely because the arm agrees with the correct answer. ARM E, the term that was MISSED by the first four and found by decomposing a claim that failed for the wrong reason: a consumer importing std.integer AND v2.std.integer DIRECTLY emits BOTH use-lines and is CORRECT. So the emitter is right whenever the author named both providers, and the defect needs the second declaration to arrive unnamed.",

    "HOW ARM E WAS FOUND, recorded because the near-miss is the lesson. The first enrolled control encoded the condition as arms A-D stated it and asserted the use-line ABSENT for a single synthetic module importing both providers. It returned false. A Bool over a conjunction -- compiles clean AND every include present AND every exclude absent -- names none of its three conditions, so the verdict was decomposed into four separate probes rather than explained: the source compiled clean, spelled UInt8, AND CARRIED BOTH USE-LINES. The claim had been failing because the defect does not reproduce in that shape, not because of a spelling error in the assertion. HAD IT BEEN LEFT AS AN EXPECTING-RED CLAIM IT WOULD HAVE BEEN GREEN-BY-ACCIDENT ONLY AFTER A FUTURE REPAIR, and red meanwhile for a reason unrelated to the defect it names.",

    "WHERE THE DIVERGENCE IS, AS A RELATION RATHER THAN A LOCATION. The resolver and the emitter answer the same question -- which declaration does this authored import name -- and disagree. The RESOLVER IS THE CORRECT SIDE and it already carries a working wall: an UNIMPORTED bare reference to a forked spelling refuses at resolve with `ambiguous reference 'UInt8': 2 candidates: std.integer.UInt8, v2.std.integer.UInt8 -- qualify by containment path, alias, or rename`, measured in this same session. That wall correctly stays SILENT for base16, because the name is carried by an authored import and is therefore not ambiguous at resolve. The emitted use-line is then derived through a BARE-NAME lookup that can cross to the other declarer, and for a name with no nominal shell the crossing ends in no line at all. Two authorities for one question, which is DESIGN section 3's fork, with the emitter on the wrong side of it.",

    "WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CLAIMED: which predicate decides it. The arms establish the CONDITION -- and that emission and resolution disagree -- not the mechanism. `v1.compiler.emit_rust` emit_specific_import_block filters a name through is_import_graph_type_name and then imported_name_is_non_emittable_type, and both consult a defining module rather than the authored provider, which is consistent with every arm; that is a READING and it has no discriminating red, so it is written here as a reading and not as the located cause.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable -- the failure occurs and the harm is contained only because the TARGET compiler refuses. Nothing in gunbc refuses: the emission reports clean. ATTAINABLE CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed -- which declaration an authored import names is fully modeled and decidable at emission, because the resolver already decides it for the same module; a single authority makes the disagreement unwritable rather than checked. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, at capability grain: THE EMITTED USE-LINE FOR AN AUTHORED IMPORT NAME IS DERIVED FROM THE RESOLVER'S BINDING FOR THAT NAME IN THAT MODULE, rather than from a bare-name lookup over the closure -- SUFFICIENT FOR emission and resolution to name the same declaration for every authored import, so an emitted module cannot spell a type it declined to import. Deliberately NOT written as 'locate the filter' or 'fix emit_specific_import_block': a trigger naming an investigation is retired by the investigation while the defect stands, and a trigger naming one predicate is satisfied by editing it while the second authority survives.",

    "THE ENROLLED CONTROL, and it is the whole reason this row is filed rather than left for rediscovery. test.claim.authored_import_use_line_binding_witness_test drives the real emitter over both poles plus the nominal bound. Its expecting-red arm asserts the use-line is ABSENT today; when the repair lands that arm GOES RED, and per DESIGN section 4b(4) it flips to a permanent regression control -- move the string from excludes to includes -- rather than retiring. THIS DEFECT HAS NO SYMPTOM ANY MORE: the site that exposed it, extdeps.numeric.base16, was repaired at the source by the sealed-carrier change in the same PR, so the E0425 is gone while the defect is untouched. A latent defect WITH a discriminating red is in a better position than one with a symptom and no red, but only because the red exists.",

    "TWO INVALID INFERENCES WERE MADE AND CORRECTED WHILE THIS WAS BEING ESTABLISHED, recorded because both are cheap to repeat. FIRST: 'adding the suggested use-line to the emitted artifact does not make it compile, therefore the use-line was not dropped.' That does not follow -- the line being DROPPABLE and the line being SUFFICIENT are independent facts, and here both defects were live at one site, so repairing neither alone compiles. The general form, adopted as a review rule: WHEN AN EXPERIMENT CHANGES ONE VARIABLE AND THE SYMPTOM PERSISTS, THE CONCLUSION IS NOT-SUFFICIENT, NEVER NOT-CAUSAL; establishing non-causality needs a discriminator over the condition itself. SECOND: 'rustc offers two candidates, therefore the emitter faced an ambiguity.' The `help:` list is generated from the crate symbol table AFTER resolution fails and enumerates every candidate present, so it is consistent with an unambiguous dropped line and discriminates nothing on its own.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: an E0425 or E0433 in an emitted module for a bare type name whose SOURCE module imports exactly one provider for it, with rustc offering two or more `use crate::...` candidates. Two cheap discriminators, and neither is adding the suggested import, which answers a different question: re-emit with the second declaring module out of the closure and see whether the line returns (arm C), or add a direct import of the second provider to the consumer and see whether BOTH lines appear (arm E). THE WORKAROUND FALLS OUT OF ARM E -- naming the second provider in the consumer's own import list restores the line -- but it is a workaround and not the repair, because it requires every consumer of a forked alias spelling to name a module it does not otherwise use.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "emit_specific_import_block", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v1.compiler.emit_rust", decl_name: "imported_name_is_non_emittable_type", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.integer", decl_name: "UInt8", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.termination", decl_name: "TerminationProof", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
